<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/10th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/911">911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, traditionally dated to 911, was an agreement between the West Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a>. Charles granted Rollo lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in return for defending them against other raiders and accepting baptism. The grant was the origin of the Duchy of Normandy, although no text of the agreement survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-clair-sur-epte.jpg" alt="Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Alexandrin at French Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_%C3%A0_la_fin_du_Xe_si%C3%A8cle.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> had raided Frankish lands since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s time, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 845 and 885, and Carolingian kings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> onwards failed to stop them by force or by payment. In time the counts and dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> fortified their own lands, and raiding grew harder. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a>, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dudo-of-saint-quentin">Dudo of Saint-Quentin</a>, had fought a rebellion against the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> before he became one of the leaders of the Viking Siege of Paris in 885 and 886. By 900 his followers had settled along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>.</p><p>In June 911 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> without success, and on 20 July the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> under Robert, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, later Robert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, relieved the town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a>, who was with the army, then chose to negotiate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> refused an offer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and asked for better farmland. The extent of the grant west of the Epte is debated, but the consensus is that it was broadly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> and present-day Upper Normandy, possibly the pagi of the Caux, Évrecin, Roumois and Talou. Rollo pledged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>, vowed to guard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> estuaries, and took the baptismal name Robert after his godfather. Some authors argue the land was granted outright, with no oath owed. The deal was to be sealed by a marriage to Gisela, daughter of Charles.</p><p>The terms are known only through Dudo, writing a century later, and the agreement was probably verbal and likely made in the autumn. A royal diploma of 918 confirms the grant. Dudo's tale of a warrior toppling the king while kissing his foot is likely exaggerated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> shared the lands between the Epte and the Risle among his chieftains and settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>. Their descendants adopted the local Gallo-Romance language, married into the native population and took up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and the Northmen became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, though the first settlers also brought slaves, mainly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>. Rollo's successors extended the domain to the Cotentin and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pays-de-caux">Pays de Caux</a>. Charles himself was deposed in 923, and Rollo's descendant William became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> in 1066.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The agreement is traditionally called the <b>Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte</b>, although no text survives and its terms are known mainly from the later account of Dudo of Saint-Quentin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The failure led to the <b>Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte</b>, by which King Charles the Simple granted Rollo lands around Rouen.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-20">20 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
